## Why This Is Asked
Tests for real-time collaboration with CRDTs are complex due to concurrent edits and network churn. This question probes end-to-end orchestration, deterministic testing, and conflict-resolution validation.

## Key Concepts
- CRDT-based synchronization across two clients
- WebSocket messaging and presence propagation
- Offline edits and merge semantics
- Test determinism via mocked timelines and seeded data

## Code Example
```javascript
const { test, chromium } = require('@playwright/test');

test('collab doc CRDT merge with offline edits', async ({ browser }) => {
  const ctxA = await browser.newContext();
  const ctxB = await browser.newContext();

  const pageA = await ctxA.newPage();
  const pageB = await ctxB.newPage();

  await pageA.goto('/doc/seeded-doc-id');
  await pageB.goto('/doc/seeded-doc-id');

  // Verify presence
  await expect(pageA.locator('.user-list')).toContainText('User B');
  await expect(pageB.locator('.user-list')).toContainText('User A');

  // Concurrent edits
  await pageA.locator('.editor').type('Hello ');
  await pageB.locator('.editor').type('World');

  // Verify CRDT merge (order may vary based on timestamps)
  await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText(/Hello World/);

  // Simulate disconnect
  await pageB.evaluate(() => window.mockWebSocket.close());
  await pageB.locator('.editor').type(' from offline');

  // Reconnect and verify merge
  await pageB.evaluate(() => window.mockWebSocket.reconnect());
  await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText(/Hello World from offline/);

  // Verify no duplicate edits
  const textA = await pageA.locator('.editor').textContent();
  const textB = await pageB.locator('.editor').textContent();
  expect(textA).toBe(textB);
});
```

**Answer: Playwright** — superior isolation with contexts, better mocking capabilities via browser automation, and more reliable for multi-tab/session scenarios.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests around feature flags in E2E flows are common in production environments. This evaluates the ability to design deterministic tests for both flag states, validate calculations, and ensure order completion under different configurations.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags and gated flows
- API-driven data seeding for test setup
- Network and service mocking for external dependencies such as tax and fraud services
- Deterministic assertions and test isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test skeleton
async function testFlag(flagValue) {
  // Toggle feature flag via API
  await api.post('/api/feature-flags', { flag: 'new-checkout', enabled: flagValue });

  // Seed cart and user
  await api.post('/api/test/seed', { userId: 'user-123', items: [...] });

  // Mock external services
  await page.route('**/api/tax*', route => route.fulfill({ body: { rate: flagValue ? 0.085 : 0 } }));
  await page.route('**/api/fraud*', route => route.fulfill({ body: { status: 'pass' } }));

  // Navigate to checkout and complete flow
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await page.click('#place-order');

  // Assert final total and order completion
  const finalTotal = await page.textContent('#total');
  expect(finalTotal).toBe(flagValue ? '$108.50' : '$100.00');
  expect(await page.locator('.order-success')).toBeVisible();
}

// Run tests for both flag states
test('Legacy checkout', () => testFlag(false));
test('New checkout', () => testFlag(true));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle rate limiting on the feature flag API?
- What strategies would you use to debug flaky tests in the fraud check flow?
- How do you ensure test data isolation between parallel test runs?

